.TH INCOGNITO-TERMINAL 1 "2026-10-15" "Incognito Terminal 1.2.4" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
incognito-terminal \- run a terminal session with display masking and history
controls
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B incognito-terminal
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ --
.IR shell
.RI [ arguments ...]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B incognito-terminal
is a user-space PTY wrapper.  It passes input to the child shell unchanged,
reconstructs the terminal display, and masks values associated with configured
triggers before drawing them on the outer terminal.
.PP
Press F2 inside a session to configure masking triggers, command exceptions,
the privacy state, display style, and the history preference for future
sessions.
.PP
P opens the Privacy submenu. Select On, Off, or Crazy with Up/Down or O/F/C,
then press Enter to apply; Esc cancels. On masks configured sensitive values;
Off shows original text; Crazy masks all Bash/Zsh command input, including
command exceptions, while keeping prompts, whitespace, and command output
visible.
The status bar and menu remain readable. Sessions start On.
.PP
M opens Privacy mode: Asterisks, Obfuscation, or Emoji. The style is saved and
applies to On and Crazy. Asterisks are green. Random characters and yellow
smiling faces remain stable on redraw. Emoji masking preserves terminal column
positions, using a narrow smiley for an odd column.
.PP
S opens Shortcuts. Edit the Mask next value or Hold to reveal binding as text,
then press Enter to save. Defaults are Alt+I and Alt+U.
Ctrl+M cannot be assigned because terminals encode it as Enter.
Manual masking skips leading spaces and preserves shell quote delimiters.
It uses the selected display style independently of automatic triggers,
command exceptions, or Privacy Off. The current value ends at its shell
boundary.
.PP
Hold reveal exposes concealed text only until release or focus loss.  Enhanced
terminals use exact release events.  On legacy terminals, key repeat keeps the
text visible and it is concealed shortly after repeat stops; a tap auto-hides.
.PP
In Triggers and Exceptions, Space enables or disables the entire list without
removing entries. List switches and shortcut bindings persist across restarts.
.PP
For a trigger whose value follows whitespace, a shell backslash-newline
continuation and indentation before the value are handled automatically.  This
behavior is always active and does not change the command sent to Bash or Zsh.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --clean
Skip Bash or Zsh user startup files for this session.
.TP
.BI --history " normal|session|off"
Override the saved history preference for this launch.  Normal delegates to the
shell's existing behavior.  Session keeps recall in memory but does not save it
to the normal history file.  Off disables recall after each completed command
and does not save commands to the normal history file.
.TP
.BR -h ", " --help
Show command-line help and exit.
.TP
.B --version
Show the program version and exit.
.TP
.B --
End wrapper options.  Remaining arguments select the shell and its arguments.
If no shell is given, the program uses
.B $SHELL
or falls back to
.BR /bin/bash .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/incognito-terminal/config.json
Per-user settings when XDG_CONFIG_HOME is set.
.TP
.I ~/.config/incognito-terminal/config.json
Default per-user settings location.
.SH LIMITATIONS
Display masking does not alter command arguments.  Sensitive values may remain
visible through process inspection, application logs, nested sessions, or other
channels.  History modes control supported Bash and Zsh sessions only.  They do
not erase existing history files or control independent history services.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The wrapper normally returns the child shell's exit status.  Startup or terminal
setup failures return a nonzero status.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bash (1),
.BR zsh (1)
